Certifications can get expensive fast, especially when you are paying out of pocket as a student. A single exam voucher often costs $99 to $300, which is hard to justify before you are earning full-time.

Instead of paying full price, I started hunting for student programs, free trial attempts, and community badge challenges that cover the exam fees. Here is how I am stacking **GitHub**, **SAP**, and **AWS** certifications for free, starting with my GitHub Foundations exam tomorrow.

## 1. GitHub Foundations (via the Student Developer Pack)

The standard fee for the **GitHub Foundations** exam is $99. Because I am a student, I applied for the **GitHub Student Developer Pack** first. Once my student status was verified, it unlocked a free certification voucher along with partner learning tools.

To prepare, I kept my study routine practical:

1. **Microsoft Learn.** The official GitHub Foundations path maps straight to the seven exam domains. I used it as a checklist for policy details like repository permission roles, branch rulesets, and enterprise settings.
2. **DataCamp.** Included through the Student Pack. The short interactive exercises helped reinforce Git commands and branching workflows quickly.
3. **A two-account sandbox.** Reading documentation is not enough, so I built a practice repository and used a second GitHub account to simulate a real team. I forked the repo, triggered and resolved merge conflicts, closed issues with keywords like `Closes #1`, wrote YAML issue forms, and set up a GitHub Actions workflow.

## 2. SAP Certifications (two free student trials)

Enterprise software certifications usually cost hundreds of dollars, so I was surprised to find that **SAP** gives students **two free certification exam attempts** through their student program.

Having two attempts takes a lot of pressure off. It means I can use official SAP Learning Hub student resources to prepare for my first enterprise cloud or development exam, sit for it, and still have a backup attempt in reserve without risking my own money.

## 3. AWS Certifications (AWS Builder Center streaks and badges)

For cloud, I am working toward a free **AWS** certification voucher through the **AWS Builder Center** community challenges. Instead of a one-click student discount, AWS rewards consistent participation with digital badges that unlock voucher opportunities.

Right now, my progress looks like this:

- **30-day streak:** Completed today. Logging in and engaging with AWS Builder Center resources every day for a month unlocked my first consistency milestone.
- **90-day streak:** In progress. Keeping the daily habit going for another two months is the next target on the board.
- **Meaningful Contributor badge:** Next on my list. Beyond showing up daily, this requires sharing helpful posts, guides, or answers that actually help other builders in the community.

## Setting up my laptop for OnVUE tomorrow

I am sitting for the **GitHub Foundations** exam tomorrow from home on my laptop through Pearson VUE's **OnVUE** online proctoring. Online proctored exams have strict rules, so I set everything up today:

- **System check.** Installed the OnVUE app and tested my webcam, microphone, and internet stability ahead of time.
- **Clean desk.** Cleared everything off my table except my laptop and my government ID. No second screens, no notebooks, and no phone within reach.
- **Quiet window.** Booked a time slot when the house is quiet so background noise will not interrupt the session.

---

If you are a student, check for student packs, trial vouchers, and community badge tracks before ever paying retail price for a certification.
